title op3: children that left, under all three emigrant policies

# Successor question S1, built so the three policies on the table
# produce three different pictures rather than three descriptions of
# one.  A fixture that documents a question is worth less than one
# that answers it, and S1 is a decision the lead has to make.
#
# Two folders, both rebuilt rather than renamed, both losing children
# to somewhere else.  Every file keeps its dnode, so red says exactly
# where each one went; only the directories are severed.  Onto is
# quiet throughout, so nothing here is about conflict.
#
#   one-out/   three children, ONE leaves.  p and q land in the
#              rebuilt one-in; r moves to /attic.
#   two-out/   three children, TWO leave, and to DIFFERENT places.
#              s lands in the rebuilt two-in; t moves to /attic and
#              u to /shed.
#
# THE THREE POLICIES.
#
#   Unanimity among voters (v1, adopted, and what the gold below
#   records).  An emigrant blocks.  Both folders are blocked, so both
#   directories die and are reborn.  The two files that stayed are
#   relinked under a fresh directory dnode.
#
#   Emigrants abstain (git's spirit).  An emigrant is simply not
#   counted.  one-out has voters p and q, both for one-in: believed.
#   two-out has one voter, s, for two-in: believed.  BOTH move, and
#   both directories keep onto's dnode.
#
#   Majority (git's letter).  one-out: two of three for one-in, so
#   believed.  two-out: one for two-in, one for attic, one for shed,
#   so no target holds a majority and it is blocked.  ONE moves.
#
# So the three policies give blocked/blocked, moved/moved, and
# moved/blocked.  No two agree on this fixture, which is the whole
# reason it has two folders instead of one.
#
# WHAT THE CHOICE COSTS is small and worth stating so the decision is
# not made on fear.  Red pairs every file pool whatever is decided,
# so no file materializes and no content moves anywhere unexpected
# under any of the three.  The entire difference is whether a
# DIRECTORY keeps its dnode.  Unanimity is the conservative reading
# and it is the one the document's exact-evidence character argues
# for; it is also the one that throws away a directory identity
# because somebody moved one file out of a folder they were
# reorganising, which is not a rare thing to do.
#
# WORKED OUTCOME (by hand, before any implementation ran)
#   believed moves:  none, under v1.  /one-out is blocked by r, whose
#                    red edge points into /attic; /two-out is blocked
#                    by t and u.  Both have candidates and refuse
#                    them -- contrast op3-severed-cross-parent, where
#                    there is nothing to refuse
#   deltas:          no pool has a lost or gained set; every file
#                    keeps its single name and changes its parent
#   decisions:       /one-out and /two-out die; /one-in and /two-in
#                    are born fresh; p, q, r, s, t, u all keep their
#                    dnodes and are relinked where red says
#   conflicts:       none, onto is quiet
#   emission:        link the new directories, rename each file into
#                    place (same pool, so a move, not unlink-plus-
#                    link), then unlink the emptied directories --
#                    children first for the removals
#
# UNDER THE OTHER TWO DIALS the gold changes in exactly one way per
# folder: 30-300 becomes 4-100 when one-out is believed, and 31-300
# becomes 5-100 when two-out is.  Nothing else in the tree moves.

tree base
  1-1: /
    2-100: attic/
      9-100: .keep = kv0
    3-100: shed/
      8-100: .keep = sv0
    4-100: one-out/
      10-100: p = pv0
      11-100: q = qv0
      12-100: r = rv0
    5-100: two-out/
      20-100: s = sv0
      21-100: t = tv0
      22-100: u = uv0

tree onto
  1-1: /
    2-100: attic/
      9-100: .keep = kv0
    3-100: shed/
      8-100: .keep = sv0
    4-100: one-out/
      10-100: p = pv0
      11-100: q = qv0
      12-100: r = rv0
    5-100: two-out/
      20-100: s = sv0
      21-100: t = tv0
      22-100: u = uv0

tree offof
  1-1: /
    2-100: attic/
      9-100: .keep = kv0
      12-100: r = rv0
      21-100: t = tv0
    3-100: shed/
      8-100: .keep = sv0
      22-100: u = uv0
    30-300: one-in/
      10-100: p = pv0
      11-100: q = qv0
    31-300: two-in/
      20-100: s = sv0

# --- expected result, worked by hand 2026-08-31 ---
expect clean

tree expected
  1-1: /
    2-100: attic
      9-100: .keep = kv0
      12-100: r = rv0
      21-100: t = tv0
    30-300: one-in
      10-100: p = pv0
      11-100: q = qv0
    3-100: shed
      8-100: .keep = sv0
      22-100: u = uv0
    31-300: two-in
      20-100: s = sv0
